HST Office: the office never writes Python bytecode into its installation; on Windows and Linux it
keeps a bytecode cache in the user profile instead.

Without this an HST Office.app the user can write to (an admin's /Applications) gets __pycache__
folders and rewritten stale .pyc files inside the bundle on every start, and
codesign --verify --deep --strict fails afterwards. Upstream's HAVE_FEATURE_READONLY_INSTALLSET
branch sets PyConfig.write_bytecode = 0 on macOS, but that PyConfig is never passed to Python
(Py_Initialize() is called, not Py_InitializeFromConfig()). Environment variables are read by
Py_Initialize() and inherited by the helper processes the office starts with the bundled python3
(the update helper, the USB token worker). A value already set is kept.
- macOS: PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1; the bundle ships checked bytecode (precompile-python.sh).
- Windows and Linux: the installers ship no bytecode, and with PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 every start
  compiled each module of the HST extension and the standard library again while the splash screen
  was up (scripts/perf/startup-timing.py). PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX=<user installation>/cache/python makes
  Python write the bytecode there once and read it on later starts; nothing is written next to the
  installed sources. PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 when the profile path cannot be resolved.

--- a/pyuno/source/loader/pyuno_loader.cxx
+++ b/pyuno/source/loader/pyuno_loader.cxx
@@ -238,6 +238,35 @@
     o3tl::setEnvironment(u"PATH"_ustr, sPath);
 #endif
 
+    // HST Office: Python never writes bytecode into the installation. An HST Office.app the user can write
+    // to (an admin's /Applications) would get __pycache__ folders and rewritten .pyc files, which breaks the
+    // bundle's code signature seal; a per-machine Windows or Linux installation would keep them after an
+    // uninstall. Py_Initialize() below reads the environment (the PyConfig above is not passed to it, so its
+    // write_bytecode is not used), and every process the office starts with the bundled python3 (the update
+    // helper, the USB token worker) inherits it. A value already set is kept.
+    if (o3tl::getEnvironment(u"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE"_ustr).isEmpty()
+        && o3tl::getEnvironment(u"PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX"_ustr).isEmpty())
+    {
+#ifdef MACOSX
+        // The app bundle ships checked bytecode (scripts/macos/precompile-python.sh); a cache folder
+        // elsewhere would make Python look past it.
+        o3tl::setEnvironment(u"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE"_ustr, u"1"_ustr);
+#else
+        // Windows and Linux installations ship no bytecode, so without a cache every start compiled each
+        // module again while the splash screen was up (scripts/perf/startup-timing.py). Python keeps it in
+        // the user profile's cache folder, next to the office's own caches.
+        OUString aCache(u"${$BRAND_BASE_DIR/" LIBO_ETC_FOLDER "/" SAL_CONFIGFILE("bootstrap")
+                        ":UserInstallation}/cache/python"_ustr);
+        rtl::Bootstrap::expandMacros(aCache);
+        OUString aCachePath;
+        if (osl::FileBase::getSystemPathFromFileURL(aCache, aCachePath) == osl::FileBase::E_None
+            && !aCachePath.isEmpty())
+            o3tl::setEnvironment(u"PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX"_ustr, aCachePath);
+        else
+            o3tl::setEnvironment(u"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE"_ustr, u"1"_ustr);
+#endif
+    }
+
     PyImport_AppendInittab( "pyuno", PyInit_pyuno );
 
 #if HAVE_FEATURE_READONLY_INSTALLSET
